<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  FRED's privacy manifest.

  WHAT THIS FILE COVERS: the analytics this repository's analytics layer sends, and the
  required-reason APIs the app itself calls. It is written to agree, line for line, with
  FREDdocs/what-we-send.md and FREDdocs/privacy-manifest-and-label.md. Change one and change all
  three.

  WHAT IS NOT DECLARED HERE, AND MUST BE BEFORE SUBMISSION: the data the KYC and banking flows
  collect, which is name, date of birth, Social Security number, address, email and bank account
  details. That data is collected by those flows and sent to our own servers and to our broker,
  not by analytics, and whoever owns those flows has to declare it. A manifest that covers only
  analytics is incomplete for the app as a whole. FREDdocs/privacy-manifest-and-label.md names
  exactly which entries are still owed and who owns them.

  Why the identifier is NOT linked: the only identifier analytics sends is `public_id`, a random
  UUID generated for analytics and nothing else. It is not the row id, not the email address, not
  the broker account id, and it cannot be reversed into any of them from outside our database.
  Nothing is sent to an advertising or attribution SDK; there is none in the app. NSPrivacyTracking
  is false for the same reason: no data is shared with a data broker and nothing is joined to
  third-party data to target anyone.

  WHAT WOULD MAKE THAT FALSE: attaching anything that identifies the person to a person record, or
  sending revenue or purchase data joined to the user. Billing does not exist in this repository
  yet. The day it does, this file has to be revisited before the build ships. See J5 in
  FREDdocs/analytics-implementation-review.md.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No tracking: nothing is shared with a data broker, and nothing is joined to third-party
	     data for advertising or measurement across apps. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Which screens were reached, which steps were completed, which controls were used.
		     Banded, never a raw amount and never free text. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- The opaque analytics UUID (`public_id`). Declared as a User ID because it is stable
		     per account, and NOT linked because it exists only to group one person's events and
		     cannot identify them outside our own database. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Capacitor's own bridge stores preferences in UserDefaults, and the WebView's local
		     storage sits behind it. CA92.1 is "access information from the app itself", which is
		     the only use here: nothing reads another app's defaults and nothing is sent anywhere.
		     Declared by the app because the Capacitor pods, which do ship manifests, declare an
		     EMPTY NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes in them. An empty declaration is not a declaration,
		     so if the app does not make this one, nobody does. FRED's own Swift code does not
		     touch UserDefaults; this covers the bridge underneath it. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
